@ 17.12.1.1.1 - Classical Latin vs. Vulgar Latin
# citation: “The following relate to the changes of vowels as found in the evolution to the written medieval languages of Iberia, Gallia and Italia (Anglo-Norman, Old Spanish, etc.). The Latin of Africa, Sardinia and the easternmost parts of the Empire exhibited different mergers.”
	{e,i} > j / C_V when unstressed
	{e,ai} > ɛ
	{i,e:[+long],oi} > e
	i:[+long] > i
	o > ɔ
	{u,o:[+long]} > o
	u:[+long] > u
	a:[+long] > a
	m > n / _# (in certain common monosyllabic words, as well as some common compounds of them)
	m > ∅ / _#
	h > ∅
	w > β
	{ ɛ, ɔ } > { e, o } / when unstressed
	j > ʝ / #_V
	j > ʝ:[+long] / V_V
	{ “In, contrast,, Somanian, exhibit͡s, u,, u:[+long] } > { u, (and, ultimately, also, ɔ,, o:[+long] }
	u (and ultimately also ɔ, o:[+long] > o); and Sardinian and:[+affricate]frican Latin underwent a straight merger of the vowels by length without considering quality (e, e:[+long]
	o); and Sardinian and:[+affricate]frican Latin underwent a straight merger of the vowels by length without considering quality (e, e:[+long] > e; i, i:[+long]
	{ e;, i,, i:[+long] } > { i;, u,, u:[+long] }
	i; u, u:[+long] > u; etc.)”